{
  "title": [
    "svfilter2~"
  ],
  "description": "zero-delay feedback state-variable filter",
  "origin": "ELSE",
  "categories": [
    "Filters"
  ],
  "inlets": [
    {
      "messages": [
        {
          "type": "signals",
          "description": "signal(s) to be filtered"
        }
      ],
      "repeat": false
    },
    {
      "messages": [
        {
          "type": "float/signals",
          "description": "sets cutoff/center frequency value(s)"
        }
      ],
      "repeat": false
    },
    {
      "messages": [
        {
          "type": "float/signals",
          "description": "sets Q/resonance value(s)"
        }
      ],
      "repeat": false
    }
  ],
  "outlets": [
    {
      "messages": [
        {
          "type": "signals",
          "description": "lowpass filter signal(s)"
        }
      ],
      "repeat": false
    },
    {
      "messages": [
        {
          "type": "signals",
          "description": "highpass filter signal(s)"
        }
      ],
      "repeat": false
    },
    {
      "messages": [
        {
          "type": "signals",
          "description": "bandpass filter signal(s)"
        }
      ],
      "repeat": false
    },
    {
      "messages": [
        {
          "type": "signals",
          "description": "notch filter signal(s)"
        }
      ],
      "repeat": false
    }
  ],
  "arguments": [
    {
      "type": "float",
      "description": "cutoff/center frequency (Hz)",
      "default": "0"
    },
    {
      "type": "float",
      "description": "Q/resonance",
      "default": "0.01"
    }
  ],
  "flags": [],
  "methods": [
    {
      "type": "clear",
      "description": "clears the filter in case of a blow-up"
    }
  ],
  "body": "[svfilter2~] is a zero-delay feedback state-variable filter. It is a more precise alternative to [svfilter~], with accurate cutoff tuning across the full frequency range, unconditional stability and resonance given directly in true Q units.\n\nThe filter outputs the same number of channels as the left input. If the right inputs have more than one channel, the number of channels must match the left input. A single channel in the secondary inlets gets repeated to match the channels of the left input."
}